  "plot_summary": "Loki, banished to the Isle of Silence, mentally reaches out to Earth to find a menace to confront Thor and sees the Hulk and tricks him into endangering a train so that he'll be blamed and then hunted down by Thor.&nbsp; But Rick Jones sees the news and contacts his Teen Brigade to try and summon the Fantastic Four to help find the Hulk.&nbsp; But Loki doesn't want the FF interfering, so he re-routes the radio waves toward Dr. Don Blake.\n\nBut it turns out that others have also heard the transmission, as we see Ant-Man and the Wasp heading out toward the West, where the Hulk usually is.&nbsp; And Iron Man too has picked up the summons and is able to fly himself out West as well (hours later, it says).&nbsp; Finally the FF respond to the Teen Brigade, but they're tied up with another case at the moment and can't come.&nbsp; But at that moment, Thor, Ant-Man, Wasp and Iron Man all show up to talk to Rick Jones and his friends.\n\nLoki is upset that the others are complicating things and manages to lure Thor out on his own, chasing down a mental image of the Hulk.&nbsp; Thor realizes it must be Loki and heads off to Asgard to have a look.&nbsp; But that leaves the others to track down the Hulk, and sure enough, he is spotted eventually by an ant, who reports back to Ant-Man.&nbsp; They track Hulk to the circus that he's been hiding in as a performer, and there is a brief scuffle, but Hulk manages to get free and head off.\n\nMeanwhile, in Asgard, Thor requests that Odin allow him to visit Loki on his island.&nbsp; Loki has prepared many traps of course, but eventually Thor and Loki face each other.&nbsp; But Loki's sets loose the \"Silent Ones\" - enemies of the gods!&nbsp; The Trolls!&nbsp; To make a long story short, this goes on for four pages before Thor finally grabs Loki, and curiously decides to take him back to Earth (certainly a questionable move) - \"There, you will find others who are waiting for you...others almost as powerful as <i>I</i>!\"\n\nAnd back on Earth, Iron Man is still chasing after the Hulk, who is heading toward Detroit, and they face off inside of an auto-factory.&nbsp; But Thor drops in with Loki and explains how he tried to frame the Hulk to lure Thor into battle with the behemoth.&nbsp; Loki makes himself radioactive and orders Hulk and Iron Man to flee before they're killed by the radiation, allowing he and Thor to resume hostilities, but Ant-Man and Wasp arrive on the scene, and activate a trapdoor that sends Loki down into a lead-lined tank, which will protect everyone until Loki can no longer continue, which will give Thor a chance to return him to where he belongs.\n\nBut before he departs, Ant-Man proposes that they form a team.&nbsp; \"If we combine forces, we could be almost unbeatable!\"&nbsp; Iron Man is all for it, and Thor agrees that they could do much good.&nbsp; Hulk is tired of being pursued, so he also agrees, and Jan proposes the name \"Avengers,\" which sticks.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "The 22-page story itself reads far more like a Thor solo tale, with everyone else simply getting swept up in the action.&nbsp; Thor and Loki, <i>without</i> the others, consume a <i>huge</i> chunk of the pages.&nbsp; Loki provides the catalyst to get everyone together, with the Hulk being the perfect foil.\n\nIt's not a <i>great</i> origin tale (probably below mid-grade in its own right), but it gets points for the thrill of seeing all the characters we've been reading about these last few months (save Spider-Man and the FF of course) join forces in what promises to be an interesting mix.&nbsp; I especially liked Jan's commentary on all the other members (e.g. Thor being \"dreamy\" and Iron Man \"horrible\"), as she continues to try and make Hank jealous - it's <i>completely</i> in character for her.&nbsp; All-in-all, a just-above-mid-grade rating seems about right here - solid, not to mention <i>necessary</i>, but otherwise unremarkable.\n\nHaving seen many issues now of Kirby artwork, it's clear he did the <i>full penciling</i> on this one, as everyone had that unmistakable Kirby look and treatment, leaving the inker with lighter duties this time around, as if he wanted to make sure the book got off to a good start.&nbsp; The final panel of the team together, with both Iron Man and Thor's arms raised, has, of course, been paid homage to many times over the decades since.\n\nI confess my affection and bias for the Avengers as a book, but I don't remember ever reading this story before.&nbsp; If I did, it was many decades ago, so it will be interesting starting over from the beginning like everyone else back in the Silver Age days and seeing the team morph over time.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nOne also is forced to wonder if Hulk's \"I pity the guy who tries to beat us!\" ultimately influenced the catch phrase \"I pity the fool...(<i>fill in the blank</i>)\" of a once-famous 1980's TV character!\n\n[ASSET: center | AvengersIsBorn | width=67%]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
